WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: ar

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
What should any country do to increase the money supply of its currency?

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.520
It could print paper currency at its discretion

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.160
Well, what is the situation with regard to cryptocurrencies

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:15.040
that are virtual currencies that are just codes and do not have a tangible texture

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:16.400
as mentioned in the previous episodes?

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.440
In answering this question, we will focus today on Bitcoin specifically

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.720
as the most famous, oldest and most valuable cryptocurrency

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.240
in the world today as the leader of digital currencies.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.680
How are the new bitcoins coming into existence?

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:31.520
How are bitcoins [that never existed before] being created?

00:00:31.520 --> 00:00:35.840
Do they come out of nowhere, or there must be a complicated process behind its creation?

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.760
This is what we will try to understand together in today's episode

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.640
I'm Ashraf Ibrahim, and this is our sixth episode of our new series on cryptocurrency "Crypto Economic Informant".

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.360
Well guys, there is only one way to create new bitcoins

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.520
and it is meticulously drawn and regulated by the inventor of bitcoin

00:01:07.520 --> 00:01:13.760
Satoshi Nakamoto. This process is called mining. We will talk about it in detail later.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:17.720
But in a nutshell and simply, as we mentioned before,

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.720
there is the so-called Blockchain which is a distributed database

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:27.520
in which data or transactions are stored accurately and securely in the form of blocks

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:33.560
When the block is filled with a number of transactions, it is closed and joined to the previous block chain, and so on.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:38.320
Who makes sure that bitcoin transactions are correct before putting them into a new blockchain?

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:42.960
It is the miners who do the mining operations

00:01:42.960 --> 00:01:48.040
They even compete with each other, which one is faster in confirming transactions

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:51.680
The competition itself depends on the mining power of each miner

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.200
or strength and the number of mining devices he / she has, which we will talk about in detail in the next episodes

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.600
Ok, so what do they get in return? What makes a person use very expensive mining equipment

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.160
that consumes a high amount of energy in order to verify transactions

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.320
and then put it in a block and include it in the blockchain?

00:02:09.320 --> 00:02:11.520
They do this to win two things:

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:14.760
First, the biggest and most important reward

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:21.760
the block mining reward, which is new bitcoins. Second, the fees on transactions

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:23.600
I will be discussing them separately  in another episode.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.640
So let us focus today on the reward of block mining

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:31.120
Any miner who can prepare a new block and include it in the block chain

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:34.720
takes a certain number of bitcoins as a reward

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:40.800
That is why they call it block rewards and it is the only way to extract bitcoins.

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:44.320
However, according to Satoshi Nakamoto's Bitcoin protocol,

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:51.800
the coin was designed to be available only in a limited amount, 21 million bitcoins

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:57.320
He wanted the currency to be scarce so that inflation would not occur and the value

00:02:57.320 --> 00:03:01.440
of the currency would decrease accordingly. Because as you know, any increase in the number or supply

00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:03.680
of any commodity reduces its value accordingly.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.680
The same applies to traditional currencies as well.

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:11.680
When the central bank of any country increases the supply of its local currency;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:14.480
The value of this currency will decrease and remain worthless

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.520
Therefore, Satoshi refused to offer the coin randomly,

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:21.640
preferring to have only 21 million bitcoins around the world first and foremost.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:25.160
So, how will those 21 million bitcoins come to existence?

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.400
As we said a little while ago through mining.

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:30.760
Every miner who makes a new block and joins it to the blockchain

00:03:30.760 --> 00:03:34.600
takes a number of bitcoins as a reward.

00:03:34.600 --> 00:03:40.440
Here comes a crucial point: According to the white paper published by Satoshi Nakamoto in 2008

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.080
which is the constitution of Bitcoin as we said before.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:48.360
The block reward size will be halved after every 210,000 blocks are generated.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:52.200
This number of blocks takes approximately 4 years to be mined

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:56.400
because there is a new block that is being mined every 10 minutes, as we explained before.

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:02.320
So, we can simply say that 144 blocks or blocks are mined per day

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:05.520
When bitcoin mining began in January 2009,

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.000
a miner would take a reward of 50 bitcoins for every block mined.

00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.280
Do you remember the formation block, The first block, the zero block?

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:18.520
We said that Satoshi took a reward of 50 bitcoin that time.

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:21.341
They were the first 50 bitcoins to come to existence.

00:04:21.341 --> 00:04:26.760
He took 50 bitcoins in the second block and the same in the third block, ...

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:31.920
and so in all the blocks that were included in the blockchain, up to the number 210 thousand,

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:39.280
which was mined in 2012, and at that time the number of bitcoins reached 10,500,000.

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:42.680
Then the reward was halved and became 25 bitcoins

00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.360
for each block that was mined reaching the number 420 thousand

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.720
that was mined in 2016

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.800
and at that time the number of bitcoins reached 15,750,000.

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:58.880
After that, the reward was halved to 12.5 bitcoins for each block that is mined,

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:04.240
up to the 630 thousand block that was mined in May 2020,

00:05:04.240 --> 00:05:10.440
when the number of bitcoins reached 18,375,000.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:13.840
After that, the reward was halved to 6.25 bitcoins

00:05:13.840 --> 00:05:17.880
for each block mined in the past two years, or will be mined

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:23.880
to reach block number 840 thousand, which will be mined in May 2024, God willing.

00:05:23.880 --> 00:05:30.000
This means that miners mining right now at that moment in 2022

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:35.840
are taking a 6.25 Bitcoin block reward for every block they include to the block chain.

00:05:35.840 --> 00:05:40.240
On the day this episode was written, the number of bitcoins all over the world

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.040
reached 19,060,000 bitcoins and a little change.

00:05:44.040 --> 00:05:49.280
This represents about 90% of the total number of bitcoins planned to be mined,

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:52.160
which is 21 million bitcoins, as aforementioned.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:53.800
So, are you preplixed with all these numbers? 

00:05:53.800 --> 00:05:56.720
I will summarize it for you as follows,

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.400
every 4 years the block reward is halved,

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.640
and this will continue for the month of May 2140, God willing.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:10.320
At that time, the entire 21 million bitcoins will be extracted,

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:16.440
which is the maximum number of bitcoins, and after that, the rewards for mining any block will be zero.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:20.560
You might be asking what the miners will gain in return?

00:06:20.560 --> 00:06:24.760
What is left for them whenever the rewards are zero? What is the incentive for them to continue mining?

00:06:24.760 --> 00:06:28.840
They will have the transaction fees that we mentioned quickly at the beginning of the episode,

00:06:28.840 --> 00:06:32.040
and as said, we will be discussing it in a separate episode, God willing.

00:06:32.040 --> 00:06:36.040
This was a review on the run about the block reward for miners and its system.

